## Parent
- Spec: benchmark-gepa-overhaul
- Goals: G-6
- Depends on: bench-result-substrate
- Frozen decisions: F-5, F-6

## Problem

Parent goal: Grow the benchmark with FeatureBench, the Gin render-context task, and a few cherry-picked tasks, in a format GEPA can optimize against once-run stored baselines, with a model judge for structural-tool applicability.

This curd ships the judge for G-6. With c5 it closes parent AC-4 (label half), AC-2 (critique-input half), and AC-7 (judge calls). Today nothing in the harness says which tasks structural tools should help with. `analyze.py` slices results only by capability (`benchmark/analyze.py:306-331`). Local tasks carry only `required_strings` and `forbidden_strings` ground truth (`benchmark/tasks/base.py:20-31`), so a pass or fail says nothing about whether tilth was the right tool. The row also carries grader-side text. `correctness_reason` names a missing ground-truth string (`benchmark/tasks/base.py:178-180`), and for `gin_edit_render_context` it holds held-out test output (`benchmark/tasks/gin_render_context_tasks.py:155-157`). A judge that reads the raw row would therefore leak grader inputs into reflection.

## Contract

The model judge (parent curd c4) adds a `benchmark/judge/` package and a report section in `analyze.py`. It changes no score.

- **Judge object.** `Judge(ledger: SpendLedger, client, resolve_task, *, cell_estimate_usd: float)` holds the run's shared c1 spend ledger, the `benchmark/spend.py` class `SpendLedger(max_usd)` with `would_cross(estimate)`, `charge(cost, *, source)`, and `spent` that the c1 follow-up extracts from the `run.main` locals (`benchmark/run.py:1495-1517`) and that run.py, the judge, and evolve share. `cell_estimate_usd` is the required estimate floor and has no default. `client` defaults to the `claude -p` client. `resolve_task` defaults to `judge.default_resolve_task`, which tries `tasks.TASKS.get` (`benchmark/tasks/__init__.py:80-147`) and falls back to c2's `external.resolve_task(name) -> Task | None`, which loads an admitted FeatureBench or SWE-bench Multilingual instance by instance ID. The `label` and `calibrate` CLI commands, `stripped_record`, and calibration file validation all use this default, so external instance names resolve everywhere. Its three public methods are `applicability(task) -> Label`, `critique(row, trajectory) -> str`, and `calibrate(labels) -> Agreement`.
- **Judge calls.** Every judge call runs `claude -p --output-format stream-json --verbose --model JUDGE_MODEL --tools "" --strict-mcp-config --setting-sources "" --no-session-persistence --disable-slash-commands`. The prompt goes on stdin. The call runs with its cwd set to a fresh empty temporary directory and `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` set to a fresh empty temporary directory, so no user or project settings, hooks, MCP servers, or files can load. `--strict-mcp-config` with no `--mcp-config` loads no MCP server, and `--setting-sources ""` loads no settings or hooks (`benchmark/run.py:806-812`). It never passes `--bare`, which refuses OAuth (`benchmark/run.py:806-812`). `--tools ""` disables every built-in tool (`benchmark/run.py:822-826`). The environment comes from c1's `build_runner_env` (`benchmark/run.py:205-248`) behind c1's `guard_claude_auth` (`benchmark/run.py:192-202`). `JUDGE_MODEL` is a full model ID pinned in `benchmark/judge/config.py`.
- **Labels (G-6, F-5).**
  - A label is exactly one of `strong`, `weak`, or `none`.
  - The judge answers with one token. The answer is trimmed and lowercased, then must equal a member of the set. Any other answer raises `JudgeAnswerInvalid`, writes no cache entry, and is not retried.
  - **External task inputs.** An external task exposes `problem_statement` and `gold_patch` string attributes (c2 `ExternalTask`). It is labelled from those two attributes only.
  - **Local task inputs.** A local task is labelled from its `prompt`, its `ground_truth` fields, its `test_command`, and its optional `trusted_reference` text. `gin_edit_render_context` gains a `trusted_reference` property that returns its held-out fixture test source from `HELD_OUT` (`benchmark/tasks/gin_render_context_tasks.py:17`). No other local task defines one.
  - **Cache.** The label cache key hashes c1's `task_digest`, `JUDGE_MODEL`, and the applicability prompt hash. It is separate from the run key. On a cache hit, no judge call runs. The key covers every label input because the c1 follow-up guarantees that `task_digest` hashes the task class's module source, its `check_correctness` override, and its real fixture directory. For `gin_edit_render_context` that directory is `gin_render_context_fixtures`, which holds the `trusted_reference` source. At `87f2e73` the digest hashes `<module stem>_fixtures` (`benchmark/run.py:567-581`), which misses that directory, so this curd lands after the follow-up.
- **Critiques (F-5).**
  - **One stripped record.** `judge.stripped_record(row, sidecar) -> dict` is the single stripped record for the whole system. A critique is built only from it, and c5 builds its reflective records with it rather than defining its own (parent invariant: critique inputs are that same stripped record). It holds three parts.
    - The task `prompt`, read from the task that `default_resolve_task(row["task"])` returns. For an external task this is its agent-facing prompt, never its `gold_patch` or held-out tests.
    - Allowlisted row fields: `task`, `mode`, `model`, `repetition`, `num_turns`, `num_tool_calls`, `tool_calls`, `total_cost_usd`, `duration_ms`, `result_text` (the agent final answer), `correct`, and `pass_rate`, plus every key starting `f2p_` or `p2p_` whose value is an integer.
    - The trajectory sidecar content, verbatim and untruncated, including every tool call's input and output.
  - **What is excluded.** Grader-side material never enters the record. That covers `correctness_reason`, any other row field, the task ground-truth definition (`required_strings`, `forbidden_strings`, `expected_diff_contains`), `test_command`, `trusted_reference`, gold patches, and held-out test source or output. Agent-produced text (the final answer and tool input and output) stays verbatim, even when it matches a ground-truth string.
  - **Output.** The first line of the answer must be `verdict: apt`, `verdict: missed`, or `verdict: misapplied`. Any other answer raises `CritiqueRejected` and writes no cache entry. The returned string is the whole answer.
  - **Cache.** The critique cache key hashes the row's `run_key`, `JUDGE_MODEL`, and the critique prompt hash, so a reused baseline row costs one critique only.
  - **Withholding.** `critique` raises `CritiqueWithheld` before any judge call in three cases. A row whose `contaminated` is not exactly `false` is withheld as `contaminated`. A row with no `trajectory_path` is withheld as `no-trajectory`. A call made while the current agreement is not calibrated is withheld as `uncalibrated`.
- **Calibration (G-6).**
  - **File.** The maintainer hand-labels `benchmark/judge/calibration.json`, which holds `{"tasks": {name: label}, "trajectories": [{"run_key": key, "verdict": verdict}]}`. The file ships with both sections empty, so the judge starts uncalibrated. Validation runs before any judge call and refuses the file, naming the entry and its reason, when an entry has an unknown label, an unknown critique verdict, a task name that `default_resolve_task` cannot resolve, a `run_key` with no completed stored row, a completed row with no trajectory (`trajectory_path` null or the sidecar missing), or a row whose `contaminated` is `true` or absent.
  - **Size check.** `calibrate` refuses before any judge call when the set has no task or fewer than `MIN_CALIBRATION_TRAJECTORIES` trajectories. It returns `Agreement(calibrated=False)` with the shortfall as its reason.
  - **Agreement.** Otherwise, `calibrate` labels each hand-labelled task, critiques each hand-labelled trajectory without the calibration gate (validation already admitted only clean rows with trajectories), and computes unweighted Cohen's kappa on each dimension. A rejected judge answer counts as a disagreement.
  - **Gate.** The judge is calibrated when both kappas are at least `KAPPA_THRESHOLD`. A kappa whose expected agreement is 1 is undefined, and an undefined kappa leaves the judge uncalibrated.
  - **Agreement record.** `calibrate` writes the record whether the judge is calibrated or not. It holds the judge model, both prompt hashes, the calibration file digest, both kappas, both counts, the threshold, the hand-labelled task names, `calibrated`, and `reason`.
  - **Current agreement.** The current agreement is the stored record whose model, prompt hashes, and calibration digest match the current ones. A stale record counts as uncalibrated.
- **Report slicing (G-6, F-5).**
  - **Agreement line.** `analyze.py` always adds a `## Applicability` section that reports the current agreement: both kappas, both counts, and the threshold, or the reason for uncalibrated, including `no calibration record`.
  - **Slicing.** Every row resolves to a label through its `task_digest`, `JUDGE_MODEL`, and the prompt hash. When the judge is calibrated, every task in the results is hand-labelled in the calibration set, and every row has a cached label, the section adds a Label × Mode table of correctness and cost per correct. The table uses `correctness_pct` and `cost_per_correct` (`benchmark/analyze.py:180-215`), mirroring `_capability_section`.
  - **Marked reports.** Otherwise the heading reads `## Applicability (uncalibrated)` or names the unlabelled tasks, and no table is printed.
  - **No calls.** `analyze.py` makes no judge call.
- **Spend, auth, and quota (F-6).**
  - **Ceiling.** Judge calls share the run's `SpendLedger` and its `--max-usd` ceiling. Before each uncached call, the judge computes an estimate and raises `JudgeSpendCeiling` without spawning when `ledger.would_cross(estimate)` is true. The estimate is the mean recorded cost of cached calls of the same kind (applicability or critique), else the largest judge call cost this run, else the constructor's `cell_estimate_usd` floor. So the first uncached call of a fresh run is estimated at the floor, never at 0. This rule is canonical: the `Judge` applies it inside every call, and callers, c5 included, pass the ledger and the floor but never re-define the judge-call estimate.
  - **Cost.** After each call the judge runs `ledger.charge(cost, source=...)` with the native cost when the stream carries one (`benchmark/parse.py:643`), else the pricing computation (`benchmark/parse.py:132`). A failed call is charged its native cost, else its estimate, with `source="estimate"`.
  - **Auth.** A judge call refuses to start when `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is set.
  - **Quota.** A usage-limit rejection, as classified by c1's `detect_quota_rejection` (`benchmark/parse.py:662`), raises `JudgeQuota`, caches nothing, and stops further judge calls in the process.
  - **CLI.** The `label` and `calibrate` commands require both `--max-usd` and `--cell-estimate-usd`, with no default, whenever at least one requested call is uncached. Without either flag they exit nonzero before any call. A fully cached request needs neither.
- **Score.** Labels and critiques are never written into result rows or the result store, and they never change `correct` or any existing report section (F-5).

